WEBVTT

1
00:00.960 --> 00:03.790
Your IBM i programs print invoices,

2
00:03.840 --> 00:05.850
pick lists and reports.

3
00:06.000 --> 00:09.530
Spooldoc turns those spooled files into documents.

4
00:10.460 --> 00:15.210
Your programs don't change, and nothing is installed on the IBM i.

5
00:15.260 --> 00:19.670
Spooldoc connects over the host servers and watches your output queues.

6
00:20.400 --> 00:25.190
A definition, written in YAML, reads each page into data.

7
00:25.240 --> 00:28.430
Fields by label or position, tables of lines,

8
00:28.480 --> 00:30.890
and one document per invoice.

9
00:30.960 --> 00:34.750
It reads plain text spooled files in any code page,

10
00:34.800 --> 00:37.850
and XML printed into a spooled file.

11
00:38.400 --> 00:40.910
A template lays out that data.

12
00:40.960 --> 00:43.690
It never sees lines and columns.

13
00:44.620 --> 00:48.810
Write it in HTML, or make it in Word or Excel.

14
00:48.860 --> 00:51.877
Each one makes its own file, a PDF,

15
00:51.927 --> 00:55.590
or both, and you see the document as you edit.

16
00:56.520 --> 01:01.730
From a sample spooled file, the agent proposes the definition and the template.

17
01:01.800 --> 01:04.610
You check every value next to the page.

18
01:07.607 --> 01:10.330
Each new spooled file becomes a run.

19
01:10.380 --> 01:17.290
Spooldoc reads it, makes the documents, and delivers the files to a download or a folder in the IFS.

20
01:17.340 --> 01:19.670
Every run is in the job log.

21
01:21.246 --> 01:22.616
Spooldoc.

22
01:22.766 --> 01:26.476
Spooled files into documents, for IBM i.

23
01:26.526 --> 01:29.316
Open source, MIT licensed.

24
01:29.366 --> 01:31.176
spooldoc.com.
